WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:25.179
Your pelvic organs are held in place by muscles
and tissues in your pelvis.

00:00:25.179 --> 00:00:33.860
If these muscles and tissues weaken or tear
your pelvic organs may prolapse, or drop down,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.300
into your vagina.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:42.150
If your bladder and urethra prolapse into
your front vaginal wall, you have a cystourethrocele.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:47.489
Your doctor may recommend an anterior colporrhaphy,
during which the surgeon will make an incision

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:52.850
in your vagina, push the organs back into
place and suture the tissue to help keep the

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.880
bladder in place.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:01.159
If your rectum bulges into the back vaginal
wall, you have a rectocele.

00:01:01.159 --> 00:01:05.900
You may need posterior colporrhaphy, during
which your surgeon will make an incision in

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:14.579
your vagina, push the rectum upward, and suture
the tissue to support the area.

00:01:14.579 --> 00:01:19.400
Vaginal vault suspension repairs the prolapse
of the pelvic lining and the small intestine

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.720
into the upper vaginal lining called an enterocele.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.929
Through a vaginal incision your surgeon will
push the small intestine back into position

00:01:27.929 --> 00:01:33.640
and attach the top of the vagina to strong
pelvic ligaments.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:39.719
If you have a prolapsed uterus, your surgeon
can repair it using laparoscopic instruments.

00:01:39.719 --> 00:01:44.319
Your surgeon will insert a lighted camera
into your lower abdomen to view your pelvic

00:01:44.319 --> 00:01:46.960
organs on a video screen.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.000
Using special tools, your surgeon will lift
and support your uterus by suspending it from

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.020
ligaments at the base of your spine.

